## What this is

Two crates. [`csp-policy`](crates/csp-policy) is a Content-Security-Policy as data: every
directive, source expression and token is a Rust type, and the header value is what those types
render to. [`csp-shell`](crates/csp-shell) reads the app shell your server is about to serve and
derives the `script-src` hashes from it.

Every failure mode here is silent. A browser drops a directive it cannot parse, drops a source
expression it cannot parse, keeps the rest, and reports nothing back to the origin that served it.
The header looks correct, the scripts do not run, and the page is blank. Both crates exist to move
those mistakes to a compile error, or to the point where a configuration value is read.

`csp-shell` re-exports the whole of `csp-policy`, so building a policy needs one dependency rather
than two. Neither depends on a web framework. Both are `no_std + alloc` at their core.

## Quick start

```rust
use csp_shell::{scan_shell_at, Csp};
use std::path::Path;

let scan = scan_shell_at(Path::new("dist/index.html"))?;
let policy = Csp::spa_wasm().with_scan(&scan).build();

let headers = policy.headers();
// headers.content_security_policy -> "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' 'sha256-…'; …"
// headers.cache_control            -> None, unless a per-response nonce is reserved
```

`scan_shell_at` returns a `'sha256-…'` source for every `<script>` element that carries no `src`.
`Csp::spa_wasm` is a starting policy for a WebAssembly single-page application; `Csp::new` starts
from nothing.

