## Features

- The hashes are read from the file the server is about to serve, so a hand-maintained list cannot
  drift from it. The scanner folds `\r\n` and lone `\r` to `\n` and strips a leading byte order
  mark before hashing, because both happen in the HTML parser before a script element's text
  exists, and both are invisible in the rendered header when they are wrong.
- **A per-response nonce covers script your process never sees.** Cloudflare's bot products read
  the nonce out of the `Content-Security-Policy` response header and copy it onto the inline
  script they inject at the edge, so nothing is stamped into the shell. `Policy::headers()` hands
  back the `Cache-Control: no-cache` that reserving a nonce obliges.
- Presets for seven services carry the origins each one loads from, in the directives they belong
  in: `cloudflare`, `google`, `stripe`, `sentry`, `plausible`, `fathom`, `matomo`. They compose in
  any order, are idempotent, and only ever widen a policy.
- Every term is a type rather than a string. `SourceList` is `'none'` or a list and never both,
  `HashSource` checks the length its algorithm implies, `NonceSource` enforces a 128-bit floor,
  and `Source::host` parses an origin into components, so a `;` arriving from an environment
  variable fails at the read instead of opening a directive nobody wrote.
- `'unsafe-eval'` <!-- csp-lint: allow — the routed source expressions cannot be listed without naming them -->, `'unsafe-inline'` and `'strict-dynamic'` are reached through named
  methods instead of accepted as data. A method name is a token `format!` cannot produce, so a
  lint matching `allow_unsafe_eval` in your own repository is exact where one matching a parsed
  value is a heuristic.
- **`Policy::to_header_value` returns a `String`, not a `Result`.** Every leaf type validates its
  bytes at construction, so no value in either crate can render a `;`, a `,`, a space or a control
  byte the renderer did not write itself. Seven fuzz targets and two stable-toolchain property
  tests assert it.
- `ScanResult::digest` is SHA-256 of the shell's raw bytes. That is how a caller notices a bundle
  swapped under a running process, which no configuration change announces.

## Installation

Both crates are consumed as tagged git dependencies. Nothing is published to crates.io, so there
is no version requirement to resolve: the tag names the revision.

```toml
[dependencies]
csp-shell = { git = "{{ repo.url }}", tag = "{{ shell_tag }}" }
```

Pin by tag, not branch. `Cargo.lock` records the resolved revision either way, but a branch
dependency lets `cargo update` move across arbitrary commits, whereas a tag makes every bump a
manifest edit that shows up in review.

The typed vocabulary on its own, for anything with no document to derive a policy from:

```toml
[dependencies]
csp-policy = { git = "{{ repo.url }}", tag = "{{ policy_tag }}" }
```

## Usage

### Serving the header

`Policy::headers()` returns `content_security_policy` and `cache_control`, and both belong to one
response. A per-response nonce served from cache is pinned across every reader for the lifetime of
the cache entry, which admits exactly the inline script the nonce exists to constrain.
`Policy::is_per_response()` is false when no nonce is reserved; render once at startup and reuse
the result.

There is deliberately no `tower::Layer` here. A layer you forget to mount is invisible, where a
struct field you ignore is visible at the call site, and this way the crates work under `axum`,
`actix`, `warp` or a bare `hyper` service.

### Reading an origin from configuration

```rust
use csp_shell::{Csp, Source, SourceDirective};

let cdn = Source::host(&std::env::var("CDN_ORIGIN")?)?;   // fails here, on bad configuration
let csp = Csp::spa_wasm().extend(SourceDirective::ImgSrc, [Source::SelfOrigin, cdn])?;
```

A source containing `;` would otherwise close the directive and open a new one, and the resulting
header parses cleanly in every browser.

### Adjusting a preset

```rust
use csp_shell::{Csp, DirectiveName, Scheme, Source, SourceDirective};

let csp = Csp::spa_wasm()
    // `img-src 'self' https: data:` becomes `img-src 'self' https:`
    .remove_source(SourceDirective::ImgSrc, &Source::Scheme(Scheme::Data))
    // and `font-src` falls back to `default-src` again
    .remove(DirectiveName::FontSrc);
```

Prefer removing a source to restating the list. A restated list stops tracking the preset, so a
source a later release adds is dropped with no diagnostic.

Removing every source from a directive is not the same as removing the directive. `img-src 'none'`
blocks every image; an absent `img-src` falls back to `default-src`. The two are spelled
differently because a browser treats them differently.

### Third-party services

```rust
use csp_shell::presets::{cloudflare, google, stripe};

let csp = cloudflare::script_nonce(
    google::fonts(stripe::checkout(cloudflare::turnstile(
        Csp::spa_wasm().with_scan(&scan),
    ))),
);
```

A preset whose name ends in `_nonce` reserves the per-response nonce slot and brings the
`Cache-Control` obligation with it. A preset taking an `origin` argument returns a `Result`,
because its host is deployment-specific and arrives from configuration.

## Configuration

`csp-shell` is configured at compile time, by Cargo features.

| Feature | Contents | Dependencies |
|---|---|---|
| *(core)* | `Csp`, `Policy`, `Headers`, `ScanResult`, `scan_shell`, the typed vocabulary, SHA-256 hashing | `csp-policy`, `sha2` |
| `std` (default) | `scan_shell_at`, `ScanError`, `std::error::Error` impls | — |
| `nonce` | `Nonce`, `Csp::per_response_nonce`, `Headers::nonce`, nonce splicing in `Policy::headers` | `getrandom` |
| `presets` | `presets::*`, the origins third-party services load from | — |
| `cloudflare` | `presets` and `nonce` together, under the name the Cloudflare concessions were first published as | — |

The default build compiles `csp-policy`, which has no dependencies of its own, and `sha2`.
Turning the default off leaves a `no_std + alloc` core that takes the shell's text from the
caller, usable from a build script, a bundler or a bare-metal target:

```toml
csp-shell = { git = "{{ repo.url }}", tag = "{{ shell_tag }}", default-features = false }
```

`csp-policy` has no features. It is `no_std + alloc` and dependency-free in every configuration.

